  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/8639e8b689a92b80a53fad19dd8692fdb5f1ed88a3f985927152f798d697b1b6.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?",
  "worked_solution": "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-2$ and $y=2$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-2,2),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/bbb6defd12fdc683f2df111cce7e10807fd8d69877ddf12de7fa97d3e9d53781.png)",
